---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfSecureCompressToSize from '@/components/tools/pdf/PdfSecureCompressToSize.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="compress-pdf-to-1mb">
  <PdfSecureCompressToSize client:load targetBytes={1048576} />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>A megabyte is a lot of budget</h2>
      <p>
        For most documents, compression at this target has almost no cost. A ten-page scan gets about
        100 KB a page, which is enough for 150 DPI at a JPEG quality in the seventies. At that point the
        difference from the original is invisible on screen and hard to see in print.
      </p>
      <p>
        At 1 MB, the useful question is usually why the file was 47 MB at the start.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Where the original size came from</h2>
      <p>
        PDFs are almost never large because of text. A forty-page report exported from a word processor
        is often under 300 KB in total, because each page is a few kilobytes of drawing operators plus a
        shared font. The size comes from images, mostly these:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Phone-camera scans: a modern sensor makes a 12-megapixel JPEG per page. At three or four
          megabytes each, twelve pages are 40 MB before anything else.
        </li>
        <li>
          Flatbed scans at 600 DPI: four times the pixels of 300 DPI, with no benefit unless you
          digitise artwork.
        </li>
        <li>
          Losslessly compressed images: some scanners write PNG or Flate-compressed bitmaps, which are
          much larger than a JPEG of the same visual quality.
        </li>
        <li>
          Repeated embedded fonts: merging twenty documents can embed twenty copies of the same font
          subset. This is small in comparison, but it adds up.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Rendering again at 150 DPI removes about 90 per cent of the pixels of a 600 DPI scan before the
        JPEG encoder starts. For this reason the reduction is often 95 per cent or more with no visible
        loss.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>1 MB targets you will actually meet</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Web forms built on a CMS, where 1 MB is a common default upload limit.</li>
        <li>Learning-management systems that collect coursework.</li>
        <li>Court e-filing portals, several of which specify 1 MB per exhibit.</li>
        <li>Older intranet document libraries and SharePoint list attachments.</li>
        <li>Files sent over a slow or metered connection.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Checking the result properly</h2>
      <p>
        On a document of this size, look at the per-page table below the result. Check for:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          A page with a scale below 100%. That page did not fit even at minimum quality, so its
          resolution was reduced. At a 1 MB target, this usually means the page is a full-bleed
          photograph.
        </li>
        <li>
          A wide spread of quality values. This shows the budget roll-over: a title page at 94 and a
          dense chart page at 55 is the correct behaviour.
        </li>
        <li>
          All pages at 94. This is the maximum. Your document had headroom, so a smaller target was
          possible, or no compression was necessary.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Very large source files</h2>
      <p>
        There is no upload, so the input size limit is the memory of your device, not a plan tier. In
        practice:
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Source</th>
              <th scope="col">Expect</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Up to ~100 MB, desktop browser</td>
              <td>Comfortable</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>100–500 MB, desktop browser</td>
              <td>Works; allow a minute or two and close other tabs</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Over 500 MB</td>
              <td>Desktop Chrome or Edge only, and slow</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Over ~150 MB on a phone</td>
              <td>Likely to use up the memory of the tab; split the document first</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The tool holds only one page in memory at a time, so the page count matters much less than the
        size of the image data on each page.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
